BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Make your own Hook
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261209T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261209T190000
UID:6ad68370377296003a4d1776@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Learn how to use Onshape\, a beginner-friendly 3D modeling pro
 gram\, to design your own custom hook! Explore the basics of 3D design as 
 you create a functional hook suited to your needs. Once your design is com
 plete\, library staff will 3D print it for you to pick up at a later date.
  Whether you're new to 3D modeling or curious about how 3D printing works\
 , this is a great opportunity to explore digital design and bring your ide
 as to life.\nAges 18 and up\nRegistration begins November 25 at 9:00 am.
LOCATION:Malvern\, Inside the DIH
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad68370377296003a4d1776
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Business in the Streets: The Playbook Workshop for Women
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261207T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261207T193000
UID:6ae35da5198b996a80a952e9@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Created by Business in the Streets (BITS)\, The Playbook Work
 shop is an engaging\, hands-on workshop designed to help creative women u
 nderstand how to turn their ideas into sustainable businesses. Through gu
 ided discussion\, storytelling\, and practical exercises\, participants ex
 plore what makes a business idea strong\, how to start with limited resou
 rces\, and how to find the right audience for their work.\nRegistration en
 couraged. Drop-ins welcome until capacity is reached.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Room 1
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ae35da5198b996a80a952e9
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Remapping Midlife: Finding More Fulfillment & Fun in Midlife
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261128T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261128T153000
UID:6ad77407e06996003a84b5b9@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:For 40+ year-old women who are feeling stuck in their life or 
 their career and who may not know what they want next\, you are not alone.
  This workshop offers a starting point to start reconnecting with yourself
  and find more direction and joy. It features easy exercises to gain more 
 personal and professional clarity\, celebrate strengths\, rebuild confiden
 ce and more.\nLisa Murphy is a life & wellness coach and a former media pr
 ofessional with 20+ years experience.\n*Registration required\nInformation
  Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by
  the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a
 )\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and pro
 gram registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the libra
 ry handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswer
 line@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Agincourt\, Main Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad77407e06996003a84b5b9
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ghost Stories of Toronto: Modern Phantoms
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261211T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261211T130000
UID:6a85b74ce90c830d873fe3bc@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:As Toronto has grown into a major metropolis\, its ghost stori
 es have grown along with it. Many of our city's most bone-chilling tales w
 ere born out of that rapid evolution — and the sense of unease it has of
 ten caused. We'll conclude this series of talks with author Adam Bunch by 
 exploring ghost stories from Toronto's more recent past. We'll take the su
 bway with the spectre believed to haunt the TTC\, set sail with the ghost 
 ship of Etobicoke Creek\, and meet the demon said to have terrorized one o
 f Canada's most famous folk singers.\nAbout this event's guest:\nAdam Bunc
 h\nRead More:\nThe Toronto Book of Love\nThe Toronto Book of the Dead\nAbo
 ut this series:\nYou can learn a lot about Toronto through its ghost stori
 es. Whether you believe in the supernatural or not\, the tales we tell abo
 ut spirits and spectres illuminate the true history of the city. They're o
 ften tied to dramatic stories of death and disaster\, to fascinating histo
 ric sites\, and to pivotal moments that helped shape our communities. In t
 his series of talks by author Adam Bunch\, we'll explore Toronto's past th
 rough tales of ghosts\, séances and hauntings. Whether it's phantom horse
 s from the War of 1812\, the strange prime minister who believed in the oc
 cult\, or the ghost who was arrested by the Toronto Police Morality Squad\
 , these supernatural stories have a lot to teach us about the history of t
 he place we call home.\n***\nImportant information for accessing this live
  virtual event:\nYou must register for a free ticket via Eventbrite to gai
 n access to this event.\nThis event will be presented in a Zoom Webinar. O
 nce registered\, you will receive reminder emails from both Zoom and Event
 brite with links and instructions to access your unique Zoom Webinar link 
 to watch this event.\nAt the time of the event\, you must be signed in to 
 Zoom or Eventbrite with the same email address that you used to book your 
 ticket in order to access your unique event link. Unique event links can o
 nly be open on one device at a time.\n***\nAccessibility at Toronto Public
  Library\nWe are committed to making our content accessible to as many Tor
 ontonians as possible. Please call or email us if you are Deaf or have a d
 isability and would like to request accommodation to participate in this p
 rogram. Please let us know as far in advance as possible and we will do ou
 r best to meet your request. At least three weeks' notice is preferred. Ph
 one 416-393-7099 or email accessibleservices@tpl.ca\n***\nInformation Coll
 ection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the 
 Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 2
 0(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program 
 registration\, delivery and evaluation.\nRegistrants may be contacted to f
 ill out a brief survey following the program\n. Questions about how the li
 brary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answ
 erline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b74ce90c830d873fe3bc
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Camila Mia
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261102T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261130T235959
UID:6ae91f5ef9d28050ccb0bd0d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Camila Mia is a talented young artist from Mimico\, Toronto\, 
 who has been passionate about painting since she was four. Over the years\
 , her love for art\nhas evolved into a professional pursuit\, with Camila 
 officially dedicating herself to her craft about two years ago. Her work s
 howcases a unique blend of\ncreativity and technical skill\, with a portfo
 lio that includes serene landscapes\, expressive wildlife\, and intricate 
 still-life pieces created with watercolour\,\nacrylic\, and mixed media.\n
 This exhibit is part of\nTPL's Art Exhibit\nprogram. Toronto Public Librar
 y provides space for local artists to exhibit their work at locations acro
 ss the city. These exhibits aim to reflect the diverse cultural interests 
 of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nThe exhibit can be viewed during\nRic
 hview\nbranch open hours.\nFree. All are welcome.
LOCATION:Richview
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ae91f5ef9d28050ccb0bd0d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
