DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261201T150000
UID:6adb871496ec3a003adc700d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Are you looking for subsidized housing in Toronto?\nThis sessi
 on will cover:\n- An overview of the RGI application process\n- Eligibilit
 y requirements\n- Steps for submitting and updating applications\n- Inform
 ation on priority access\n- The choice-based cycle housing offer process a
 nd the Single Offer Rule\n- Financial assistance programs to help the tena
 nts/home owners\nWhether you are a first-time applicant or seeking assista
 nce with an existing application\, this session will empower you with the 
 knowledge needed to navigate the Rent Geared to Income housing system.\nPr
 esented by The Housing Help Centre (THHC)\, a registered non-profit agency
  whose mandate is to help people access and maintain affordable and safe m
 arket rent accommodation.\nPlease arrive a few minutes early.\nThe persona
 l information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library unde
 r the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The infor
 mation will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and
  evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information can 
 be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 7
 89 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Albert Campbell\, Learning Centre on the Second Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6adb871496ec3a003adc700d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Shannon Kyles: What Our Buildings Say About Us
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261217T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261217T200000
UID:6ad7d1f59dbc2b002c8fec9d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Shannon Kyles discusses the history of architecture in Ontario
  and what it says about the people who have built this province. Starting 
 with the Indigenous people\, going through the first century of European c
 ontact\, through the 20th century wars\, democratic policies that resulted
 \, and finally onto the 21st century speculative housing practice.\nProfes
 sor Shannon Kyles is the author of ​\nThe Story of Ontario Architecture\
 n. She has written and published articles on architecture\, taught History
  of Ontario architecture for 42 years at Mohawk College and online for uni
 versities\, and has served on the board of the Architectural Conservancy o
 f Ontario for 15 years.\nRegistration encouraged for event reminders and p
 lanning. Register online at tpl.ca\, by calling 416-393-7674\, or in perso
 n at Bloor/Gladstone branch. Drop-ins also welcome.\nInformation Collectio
 n Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toron
 to Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\
 , and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program regis
 tration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles
  your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.c
 a\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Bloor/Gladstone
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad7d1f59dbc2b002c8fec9d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Artists in the Library:  Staghorn Sumac Ornament Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261205T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261205T160000
UID:6a7f617160ccaf01c01e44a3@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Part 4 of a series of workshops for adults\, exploring local f
 lora through embroidery and felt collage.  This event is part of the\nAr
 tists in the Library\nprogram\, hosted by Artist\, Sarah Zanchetta.\nRegis
 tration is required. Space is limited.\nPlease note: Registered participan
 t spaces will be held for 15 minutes after which drop-ins will be allowed.
 \nMeet the Resident:\nSarah Zanchetta (she/they) is a queer textile artist
  and educator whose practice dives into the loss of connection and knowled
 ge of poisonous plants in North America. Zanchetta uses textiles\, texts\,
  and hosts community workshops to create intimate works and spaces where w
 e can unfold our curiosities\, care and anxieties about what grows beside 
 our feet. She holds a Master of Visual Studies from the University of Tor
 onto (2023) and a BFA from OCAD University (2018).\nInformation Collection
  Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toront
 o Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\,
  and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program regist
 ration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles 
 your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca
 \n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Mount Dennis\, Room 2
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a7f617160ccaf01c01e44a3
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
