BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dignity Memorial - Funeral Planning
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261201T103000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261201T120000
UID:6adfba3eebad220045481df0@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us for a series of informative workshops designed to guid
 e you through planning meaningful funeral\, cremation\, and celebration-of
 -life services that reflect your loved one’s unique life\, values\, and 
 traditions. From personalized memorial options to pre-planning arrangement
 s\, Dignity Memorial will share helpful insights and practical information
  to help you create a heartfelt tribute that celebrates a life well lived 
 and leaves lasting memories for family and friends.\nReceive a free workbo
 ok\, spend time with other participants after the workshops\, and enjoy so
 me light refreshments provided.\nRegistration is required.\nFairview – R
 oom 201\n416-395-5750\n35 Fairview Mall Drive\, North York\, ON M2J 4S4\nI
 nformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is co
 llected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sec
 tions 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for eve
 nt and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how
  the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff 
 at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W
  2G8.\nAccessibility\nLive or automated closed captioning\, sign language 
 interpretation\, intervenor services\, or attendant services are available
  upon request. Please submit your request at least 2 weeks in advance to:\
 ntpl.ca/accessibility-events\n.
LOCATION:Fairview
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6adfba3eebad220045481df0
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dystopia Nonfiction Book Club: Regime Change
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20270208T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20270208T153000
UID:6afc888de2267c00335cfce7@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us each month to discuss issues in politics and society\,
  and explore how powerful forces are exploiting our world and dystopian im
 aginings are becoming present-day realities.\nFebruary's book is\nRegime C
 hange: Inside the Imperial Presidency of Donald Trump\nby journalists Mag
 gie Haberman and Jonathan Swan. Copies of the books are available in prin
 t\, eBook and Audiobook CD format. Book club attendees are encouraged to
  read the book in advance of the meeting.\n"Maggie Haberman and Jonathan S
 wan bring us behind the scenes of a presidency that has transformed the cu
 lture\, turned the Justice Department into an agent of retribution against
  the President's enemies and the office itself into a brazen vehicle for p
 rofit. They reveal a second term propelled by a historical irony that Trum
 p himself has come to understand: that the indictments\, the convictions\,
  the assassination attempts\, and four years of exile made him not weaker 
 but far more powerful\, more vengeful\, and more willing to gamble than an
 y President in modern history... It is also the story of something America
 n journalists are more accustomed to chronicling in distant capitals than 
 in their own: a President who has fundamentally altered the nature of the 
 office he holds—and\, with it\, how the rest of the world understands Am
 erican power."\nDrop in. No registration required.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Discussion Room\, 3rd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6afc888de2267c00335cfce7
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Artists and Entrepreneurs: How to Build a Personal Networking Circ
 le
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261212T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261212T160000
UID:6afe4a0cc58b38184c079f94@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Sustainable creative careers are rarely built in isolation. Le
 arn to map your existing relationships by identifying the people\, organiz
 ations\, and communities that can help advance your artistic goals.\nThe w
 orkshop explores how to develop relationships with peers\, mentors\, prese
 nters\, funders\, and potential collaborators while approaching each conne
 ction with reciprocity and respect.\nPresented by\nPaola Gómez\n\, an art
 ist facilitator\, community organizer\, and trained human rights lawyer. P
 aola is the Co-Founder and Program Director of\nMuse Arts\nand was previo
 usly TPL's Artrepreneur in Residence.\nFree program. This program is open 
 to everyone. Registration required.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe p
 ersonal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Librar
 y under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The
  information will only be used for event and program registration\, delive
 ry and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your informatio
 n can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-59
 81\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Locke\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6afe4a0cc58b38184c079f94
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
