BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Bootmakers of Toronto: The Sherlock Holmes Society of Canada S
 tory Meeting
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261205T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261205T160000
UID:6afe4b8ac58b38184c079fb4@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Please join The Bootmakers of Toronto for scholarship\, camara
 derie and entertainment in the social event of the holiday season.\nLongti
 me Bootmaker Bruce Aikin\, MBt.\, will present\nLook\nOut for Blondin\n\, 
 followed by special guest Mikhl Yashinsky\, who will discuss\nThe Adventur
 es of Max Spitzkopf: The Yiddish Sherlock Holmes\n. An acclaimed author\, 
 director\, actor\, educator\, Yashinsky will explore this fascinating lite
 rary detective and the cultural world from which he emerged.\nGuests can a
 lso test their Sherlockian knowledge in a trivia contest. There will be mu
 sical entertainment and the program will conclude with the Society’s Ann
 ual General Meeting.\nThere will be light refreshments plus prizes and giv
 eaways and a few more surprises. All in all\, this promises to be a pleasa
 nt\, fun-filled afternoon you won’t want to miss!\nAdmission is free. Al
 l are welcome.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall\, Ground floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6afe4b8ac58b38184c079fb4
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Parlons Français Ensemble
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T150000
UID:6aff1e546aae8314dad95573@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:« Parlons Français Ensemble » est une série interactive de
  conversations en français facilité par Pepy Malonga. À travers des dis
 cussions captivantes sur la vie quotidienne\, la culture\, la communauté\
 , le leadership et les expériences partagées\, les participants s'entra
 înent à parler français dans un cadre accueillant\, gagnent en confianc
 e\, enrichissent leur vocabulaire et tissent des liens avec les autres. Ou
 vert à tous ceux qui souhaitent améliorer leur français conversationnel
  grâce à des échanges enrichissants.\nParlons Français Ensemble is an 
 interactive French conversation series facilitated by Pepy Malonga. Throug
 h engaging discussions on everyday life\, culture\, community\, leadership
 \, and shared experiences\, participants practice spoken French in a welco
 ming environment\, build confidence\, expand their vocabulary\, and connec
 t with others. Open to participants looking to strengthen their conversati
 onal French through meaningful exchange.
LOCATION:Northern District\, Room 200
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aff1e546aae8314dad95573
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Pop-up Performance: Empire of Wild with the Canadian Opera Company
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261209T123000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261209T133000
UID:6aff5bcc4ec0691f07af7268@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:From your bookshelf to the stage\, enjoy an informal musical p
 erformance of Empire of Wild\, a new Canadian opera making its world premi
 ere at the\nCanadian Opera Company (COC)\nthis season. Based on the bestse
 lling novel by Cherie Dimaline\, the opera is composed by Métis composer 
 Ian Cusson.\nThe performance features artists of the Canadian Opera Compan
 y Ensemble Studio in excerpts of the opera.\nThe story centres on the char
 acter of Joan\, a tenacious woman who seeks to rescue her husband from the
  grips of the rougarou\, a werewolf-like creature often described in stori
 es passed down over generations. Part conversation and part performance\, 
 this event showcases the breadth and power of Métis storytelling.\nRegist
 er for a reminder. Performance starts at 12:35 pm in the main foyer of the
  branch. Experience this performance as you enter the branch. Limited seat
 s. Stay after the performance to mingle with fellow attendees to chat abou
 t the music.\nThis program is produced by Canadian Opera Company (COC).\nP
 ersonnel:\nEmma Pennell\, soprano\nAriana Maubach\, mezzo-soprano\nBen Wal
 lace\, baritone\nKimly Wang\, piano\nRepertoire:\nCecile’s Aria “You P
 oor Man”\nHeiser’s Aria “I Never Wanted to Share This”\nJoan’s A
 ria “My Prayer is Small”\n(All from Empire of Wild by Cherie Dimaline/
 Ian Cusson).\n***\nPlace a hold or borrow from Toronto Public Library:\nEm
 pire of Wild\nby Cherie Dimaline.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nT
 he personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Li
 brary under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d).
  The information will only be used for event and program registration\, de
 livery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your inform
 ation can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5
 981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Northern District\, Main Foyer
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aff5bcc4ec0691f07af7268
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Google Prompting Essentials: Online Learning Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261203T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261203T203000
UID:6af203509d27a0004556b6b2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Ready to develop the skills to use generative AI (GenAI) tools
  effectively and responsibly for work and daily life? Join our staff-facil
 itated Learning Circle to complete the self-paced Google Prompting special
 ization. By completing this Learning Circle program\, you will earn a Goo
 gle Prompting Essentials certificate to share on your resume\, LinkedIn an
 d social media profiles!\nWhat You'll Learn\nThis program is designed for 
 those with some experience with using GenAI. Through short videos\, articl
 es\, assignments and quizzes\, you will discover:\nEssential\nPrompting Sk
 ills\n: How to use a prompting framework to create a desired output with G
 enAI\; how to evaluate and refine your results.\nEveryday Work Tasks:\nStr
 ategies to draft content\, brainstorm ideas\, create tables\, timelines\, 
 and more.\nData & Presentations:\nHow to explore data visualization and us
 e GenAI responsibly when working with data.\nAdvanced Techniques:\nHow to 
 use prompt chaining for complex tasks and prompt versioning to save and re
 use your best prompts over time.\nLearning Circles are study groups guided
  by\nlibrary staff for people who want to complete the Google Prompting sp
 ecialization together in a welcoming space:\nLearners meet weekly and work
  together through course materials.\nParticipants are expected to commit t
 o attending all four Learning Circle sessions.\nSome Experience with GenAI
  Recommended: This course is ideal for participants who have completed the
  AI Essentials course Learning Circle.\nHow to Joi\nn a Learning Circle:\n
 Register once for the first session of this event series\, in order to att
 end all sessions.\nPlease sign up with a valid email address.\nA valid TPL
  library card is required for online circles.\nAttend all four sessions 
 online via Zoom\,\nThursdays December 3\, 10\, 17 from 5:30-8:30pm.\nRegi
 stration is a two-step process. First\, participants must complete their r
 egistration through this page. After this step\, library staff will follow
  up to request additional information in order to complete the registratio
 n.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form 
 is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\
 , sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used fo
 r event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions abou
 t how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline s
 taff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto O
 N M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6af203509d27a0004556b6b2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
